  0. Additional Definitions.

  As used herein, "this License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Lesser
General Public License, and the "GNU GPL" refers to version 3 of the GNU
General Public License.

  "The Library" refers to a covered work governed by this License,
other than an Application or Combined Work as defined below.

  An "Application" is any work that makes use of an interface provided
by the Library, but is not otherwise based on the Library. Defining a
subclass of a class defined by the Library is considered a mode of using
an interface provided by the Library.

  A "Combined Work" is a work produced by combining or linking an
Application with the Library.  The particular version of the Library
with which the Combined Work is made is also called the "Modified
Version".

  The "Corresponding Application Code" for a Combined Work means the
object code and/or source code for the Application, plus any data and
utility programs needed for reproducing the Combined Work from the
Application, excluding the source code for the Library.  (Thus, a user
of a Combined Work will run external processes, or use equipment to
perform such functions, except as would be expressly permitted by law
relating to invariant sections, with the object code for the Application
and the Library.)  The threshold of this criterion is that the sum of
the Library's contribution to the Combined Work is not greater in
quantity, nor greater in complexity, than an alternative open-source
implementation of these functions.

  A "User Product" is either (1) a consumer product, in which the
"information technology infrastructure" has a minimal non-removable
user-modifiable feature that is either (1) incorporated in the normal
operation of, or in packagist as part of, normal operations, or (2) is
delivered for use with, or in conjunction with normal operation of, a
User Product.  A "Standard Interface" includes any interface that
either is an official standard defined by a recognized standards body,
or, in the case of interfaces specified for a particular programming
language, one that is widely used among developers working in that
language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a Major Component, but is not part of that Major Component,
and (b) serves only to enable use of the work with that Major Component,
or to implement a Standard Interface for which an implementation is
available to the public in source code form.  A Major Component, in this
context, means a major essential component (kernel, window system, and
so on) of the specific operating system (if any) on which the executable
work runs, or a compiler used to produce the work, or an object code
interpreter used to run it.

  The "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, install, and (for an object code
work) run the object code and to modify the work, including scripts to
control those operations.  However, the Corresponding Source need not
include anything that users can regenerate automatically from other
parts of the Corresponding Source.

  The "Corresponding Source" for a work in source code form is that
same work.

  A "Modified Version" of the Library means any derivative of the
Library, as defined above.  A "Modified Version" of the Library means any
derivative of the Library, as defined above.

  A covered work means either the unmodified Library or a work based
on the Library.

  A "Modified Version" of the Library means any derivative of the
Library, as defined above.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

  A "Modified Version" of the Library means any derivative of the
Library, as defined above.

  A covered work means either the unmodified Library or a work based
on the Library.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from adapt all or part of the work
in a fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

  A library's user-facing interface includes, at its simplest, just the
sum of calling conventions for all public symbols it contains. More
broadly, a library includes the means of user-interaction, and if it is
a method of invoking a user-facing element of the library (like a menu
system), then a user-interaction feature would indeed be a substantial
part of a user-facing interface. However, simply providing a library
routine (even a system call) that performs the user-facing function is
not sufficient to fulfil the user-facing interface requirement.

  A User Product means either (1) a consumer product, in which the
"information technology infrastructure" has a minimal non-removable
user-modifiable feature that is either (1) incorporated in the normal
operation of, or in packagist as part of, normal operations, or (2) is
delivered for use with, or in conjunction with normal operation of, a
User Product.

  To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or secondarily liable for
infringement under applicable copyright law, except executing it on a
computer or modifying a private copy.  Propagation includes copying,
distribution (with or without modification), making available to the
public, and in some countries other activities.

  To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other
parties to make or receive copies.  Mere interaction with a user through
a computer network, without transfer of a copy, is not conveying.

  An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
to the extent that it includes a prominent and easily accessible
feature that clearly informs the user that no warranty is provided (except
to the extent that warranties are provided), that licensees may convey the
work under this License, and how to access a copy of this License.  For
example, if the interface is a program with a menu system, a prominent
menu item could be "Legal Notices" which displays appropriate legal
notices.  However, mere presence of a notice is not sufficient for an
interactive user interface to qualify.

